{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791706567,"nonce":"ike5rw9k3KtZXJrmscH42ELcyrI=","title":"Destroyed subtrees stay in memory: every page visited in the wiki is kept","message":"The wiki grew past 500 MB. Headless, after each round of visiting every page and returning to the overview, the JS heap grew about 21 MB and never came back (15, 38, 60, 81, 103 MB), while native node counts returned to where they were. A heap snapshot diff showed some 9,000 more HostElements, their LayoutNodes, ClassLists and texts per round; retainer paths ran Host -\u003e InlineFlows.#flows (73 of 80 sampled), Behaviours.#pictures, and Host.#nodes.\n\nHost.destroyNode called inlineFlows.forget, behaviours.forget and the owned-part teardown for the top node of the subtree only. A flow rooted deeper (every paragraph, list item and cell of a page) stayed in #flows holding its root and the subtree; an img deeper kept its picture, so its decoded image was never released; owned parts of descendants (list markers, text-field parts, a select's value and chevron) were never destroyed and stayed in #nodes.\n\nFix: destroyNode forgets every element of the subtree in its behaviours, releases its background image and destroys its owned parts; flows whose root was destroyed are dropped (InlineFlows.forgetDestroyed). The top node is still forgotten before it is unlinked, so a list renumbers.\n\nAfter: the heap stays flat across rounds (15, 17, 17, 18 MB). RSS still rises to a plateau (about 350 MB headless, 370 to 390 MB in a Retina window after every page), which is caches that do not shrink: the renderer's image atlas and the glyph atlases (see the follow-up issues).\n\nTest: tests/native-destroy.mjs (run with --expose-gc) builds and destroys pages of paragraphs, an image, a list, a field and a table, holding only WeakRefs: every node must be collectable and the layout back to its root. Before the fix 81 of 81 are kept.","files":null}]}